Using a representative beam from our electromagnetic simulations of the EIGSEP
antenna (section~\ref{subsec:eig_antenna}), a model of the horizon profile at
the EIGSEP site (Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon}), and the Global Sky Model
\citep{2017MNRAS.464.3486Z}, we simulated one sidereal day of observation by
EIGSEP over 50--250\,MHz with the forward model described in
section~\ref{subsec:fwd_modelling}.
The simulations of the beam-weighted foregrounds produce predictions for the
antenna temperature measured by EIGSEP.
In the simulations, we assumed a constant ground temperature of 300\,K. This
beam-weighted ground pickup is included in the antenna temperature and not
corrected here.

We then computed the singular value decomposition of the antenna temperature,
producing its orthogonal spectral eigenmodes, and used the resulting
eigenmodes to filter the beam-weighted foregrounds.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:singular_values}, we present the residual RMS after filtering
as a function of the number of eigenmodes filtered, $N$.
The first point -- zero modes filtered -- is the RMS of the raw waterfall
pixels about zero. This is approximately @@RMS0@@\,K.
Because the singular value decomposition is not centred, the first mode
captures the mean antenna temperature spectrum.
The residual declines steeply: with one mode filtered, the residual RMS is
@@RMS1@@\,K and with four modes filtered, the residual RMS is @@RMS4@@\,K.
The result shows that the beam-weighted foregrounds occupy a low-dimensional
subspace: filtering $N=@@NAM1@@$ orthogonal modes describes the antenna
temperature to one part in $10^{@@DYNM1@@}$, or @@FGM1@@\,mK.

To assess the spectral overlap between the beam-weighted foregrounds and the
expected cosmological signal, we applied the identical foreground filter to an
ensemble of @@NMODELS@@ global 21-cm models generated with the Zeus21
code\footnote{\url{https://github.com/ZeusCosmo/Zeus21}}
\citep{2023MNRAS.523.2587M, 2025PhRvD.111h3503C}.
The figure shows that the filter explains foreground variance more effectively
than the cosmological signal, and that after $N=@@NA@@$ modes are removed, the
RMS of the median retained 21-cm signal is larger (@@MED@@\,mK) than the RMS of
the residual foregrounds (@@FG@@\,mK).
What the filter leaves also remains informative: among the @@NDEEP@@ models
with absorption troughs deeper than 50\,mK, filtering reduces the median
separation between a pair of models over 70--130\,MHz from @@SEP0@@ to
@@SEP1@@\,mK, but @@PAIR1@@ per cent of pairs stay separated by more than
1\,mK.
While Fig.~\ref{fig:singular_values} is a useful heuristic probing the
covariance between the beam-weighted foregrounds and the 21-cm signal, it does
not represent the complete analysis needed for foreground separation.
Unmodelled instrumental systematics may require more modes filtered than in
this figure.
On the other hand, knowledge of the sky or the beam -- e.g. through beam
mapping (section~\ref{subsec:beam_mapping}) -- may allow some chromatic
structure to be modelled rather than filtered.
In practice, EIGSEP will use a differentiable forward model to evaluate
systematics and separate the foregrounds, as described in
section~\ref{subsec:fwd_modelling}.


